What is a workflow in Git/GitHub?

Answer: A workflow is the set of steps or procedures followed while developing, testing, and deploying a project using Git and GitHub. 
CI in GitHub means Continuous Integration, where code changes are automatically built and tested to ensure quality and catch errors early.
AML stands for: YAML Ain’t Markup Language

It is a human-readable data serialization language used for configuration files and automation workflows,
C:

A very popular low-level programming language.
Used for system software, operating systems, embedded systems, and performance-critical apps.
Known for speed and direct memory control.
Java:

A high-level, object-oriented programming language.
Runs on the Java Virtual Machine (JVM), so it is portable across platforms.
Common in enterprise software, Android apps, backend systems, and large applications.
JavaScript:

A scripting language mainly used for web development.
Runs in browsers to make pages interactive.
Also used on servers with Node.js.
Repository:

A repository (repo) is a storage location for project files and versions.
In Git/GitHub, it stores the code, history, branches, and commit records.
Example: GitHub repository.
Java features:

Object-oriented
Platform independent (Write Once, Run Anywhere)
Secure
Robust and reliable
Multithreading support
Automatic memory management (garbage collection)
Large ecosystem and libraries
Docker:

A tool that packages an application and everything it needs to run into a container.
Containers are lightweight and portable.
Helps in consistent deployment across different systems.
Commonly used for microservices and app deployment.
Short version:

C = system-level, fast, low-level language
Java = portable, object-oriented enterprise language
JavaScript = web/browser scripting language
Repository = project storage with version history
Java features = OOP, portability, security, multithreading, etc.
Docker = container platform for running apps consistently
